window.LA=[{"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit I - Welfare", "q": "What do you mean by Pareto Optimality? Explain the Pareto optimality criteria (marginal conditions).", "y": "2018, 2022, 2023", "a": "# Pareto Optimality\n**Meaning:** A situation is Pareto optimal when no one can be made better off without making someone else worse off. Given by Vilfredo Pareto.\n# Three marginal conditions\n- **Exchange (consumption) optimum:** MRS of X for Y must be equal for all consumers.\n[[MRS(A) xy = MRS(B) xy]]\n- **Production optimum:** MRTS of L for K must be equal for all producers.\n[[MRTS(X) lk = MRTS(Y) lk]]\n- **Product-mix (overall) optimum:** MRS = MRT (marginal rate of transformation).\n[[MRS xy = MRT xy]]\n# Explanation\n- If MRS differ, two consumers can trade and both gain, so it is not optimal.\n- If MRTS differ, shifting inputs raises output of one good without lowering other.\n- If MRS is not equal to MRT, society can change the mix of goods to raise welfare.\n# Assumptions\n- Utility is ordinal, perfect competition, no externalities, given tastes and resources.\n# Criticism\n- Silent on distribution of income, many Pareto optimal points exist.\n- Ignores interpersonal comparison, externalities, imperfect markets (second best).\n> Conclusion: Pareto criterion is a minimum efficiency test, not a full welfare test."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit I - Welfare", "q": "Critically examine the Kaldor-Hicks compensation principle. Is it superior to Pareto criterion?", "y": "2016, 2020", "a": "# Kaldor-Hicks Compensation Principle\n**Background:** Pareto criterion cannot judge a change that makes some gain and others lose. Kaldor (1939) and Hicks (1940) gave the compensation test.\n# Kaldor criterion\n- A change is an improvement if **gainers can compensate losers** and still remain better off.\n- Compensation need not be actually paid, only possible.\n# Hicks criterion\n- Change is an improvement if **losers cannot bribe gainers** to oppose the change.\n# Example\n- A new road: traders gain Rs 100, farmers lose Rs 60. Gainers can pay 60 and still keep 40. So Kaldor says improvement.\n# Merits\n- Wider than Pareto, can test policy changes (tariff, tax, projects).\n- Basis of cost-benefit analysis.\n# Criticism\n- **Scitovsky paradox:** the test may approve a move and then its reversal. So Scitovsky proposed a double test.\n- Ignores income distribution and value judgement, hypothetical compensation.\n- Cardinal gains in money, interpersonal comparison.\n> Conclusion: It is wider than Pareto but not free from value judgement, so only partially superior."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "How is a cartel formed in an oligopoly market? Explain the concept of market-sharing cartel.", "y": "2019, 2020, 2022", "a": "# Cartel\n**Meaning:** A cartel is a formal agreement among oligopoly firms to fix price or output or share the market, to act like a monopoly.\n# Types\n- **Perfect (centralised) cartel:** a central body fixes industry price, output and shares profit.\n- **Market-sharing cartel:** firms fix a common price and divide the market.\n# Perfect cartel\n- Central agency sums up marginal costs of all firms to get industry MC.\n- Equilibrium where **industry MC = MR**. Price from demand curve.\n- Output share of each firm is where its own MC equals industry MR.\n[[MC(1) = MC(2) = ... = MR (industry)]]\n# Market-sharing cartel\n- Agree on price. Share market by **non-price competition** or **quota**.\n- Quota can be on geographic area or on past sales.\n- Each firm sells at the same price but its profit depends on its cost.\n# Why cartels break\n- Incentive to cheat, entry of new firms, legal bans (antitrust), unequal cost.\n> Example: OPEC."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "What is monopolistic competition? Discuss Chamberlin's model with free entry of firms.", "y": "2018, 2020, 2023", "a": "# Monopolistic Competition (Chamberlin, 1933)\n**Meaning:** Many sellers + **product differentiation** + free entry. Mix of monopoly and competition.\n# Features\n- Large number of firms, differentiated products, selling costs, free entry and exit, downward sloping demand curve.\n# Short run equilibrium\n- Firm equates **MC = MR**. Price above AC, earns **supernormal profit**.\n[[Short run: MR = MC, P > AC]]\n# Long run (free entry)\n- Profit attracts new firms, demand curve of each firm shifts left, becomes tangent to LAC.\n- Equilibrium: **P = AC (normal profit)** but **P > MC**.\n[[Long run: MR = MC and AR = AC]]\n# Excess capacity\n- Firm produces less than minimum AC output. Gap = **excess capacity**.\n# Group equilibrium\n- Chamberlin uses DD and dd curves for individual and group demand.\n> Result: normal profit, excess capacity, wastage of resources. Criticism: selling costs, assumption of uniform demand."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "Explain Cournot's duopoly model. How is equilibrium achieved through reaction curves?", "y": "2016, 2017, 2018", "a": "# Cournot Duopoly (1838)\n**Assumptions:** Two firms, homogeneous product, zero cost (or equal), each assumes the other keeps **output constant**, market demand linear.\n# Working\n- Firm A as monopolist sells half of market demand OQ/2 in the first round.\n- B takes remaining demand and sells half of it, i.e. 1/4. A then reduces output, and so on.\n- Each adjustment reduces gap until equilibrium.\n# Equilibrium output\n[[A = OQ/3 , B = OQ/3 , Industry = 2OQ/3]]\n# Reaction curves\n- Reaction curve of A shows A's best output for each output of B (downward sloping).\n- Same for B.\n- **Intersection of the two reaction curves = Cournot equilibrium.** Both firms are in equilibrium and do not want to change.\n# Stability\n- If out of equilibrium, firms move back along reaction curves, so equilibrium is stable.\n# Criticism\n- Naive behaviour (output remains constant), no price strategy, ignores rival's reaction (Bertrand criticises).\n> With n firms industry output = n/(n+1) of competitive output."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "Explain Kinked Demand Curve theory of oligopoly.", "y": "2019", "a": "# Kinked Demand Curve (Sweezy, 1939)\n**Idea:** Explains **price rigidity** in oligopoly.\n# Assumptions\n- Few firms, if one **cuts price** rivals follow, if one **raises price** rivals do not follow.\n# Demand curve\n- Above the kink (price rise): demand is **elastic** as rivals do not follow.\n- Below the kink (price cut): demand is **inelastic** as rivals follow.\n- So the curve has a **kink** at the existing price.\n# Marginal revenue\n- MR curve has a **discontinuity (gap)** below the kink.\n- Even if MC rises or falls within gap, MC cuts MR in gap, so **price and output remain same.**\n# Criticism\n- Does not explain how the initial price is determined, evidence of price changes in practice."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit III - Consumption", "q": "Explain the Permanent Income Hypothesis and compare it with the Absolute Income hypothesis.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Permanent Income Hypothesis (Milton Friedman)\n**Main idea:** Consumption depends on **permanent (expected long-run) income**, not on current income.\n[[C(p) = k . Y(p)]]\n# Income division\n- Measured income **Y = Yp + Yt** (permanent + transitory).\n- Transitory income is saved, so it does not change consumption.\n# Features\n- k depends on interest rate, wealth ratio, tastes.\n- Long run MPC = APC. Short run MPC is lower than APC.\n# Absolute Income Hypothesis (Keynes)\n- Consumption depends on **current absolute income**. APC falls as income rises.\n# Comparison\n- Keynes: current income, short-run view, MPC < APC.\n- Friedman: permanent income, long-run view, solves **short run vs long run puzzle**.\n# Criticism\n- Permanent income cannot be measured exactly."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit III - Consumption", "q": "What are the limitations of Keynesian consumption function? Has Duesenberry given a better approach?", "y": "2017, 2022", "a": "# Limitations of Keynes consumption function\n- Considers only current income.\n- Kuznets found APC constant in long run, contrary to Keynes.\n- Ignores wealth, interest rate, past habits, demonstration effect.\n# Duesenberry's Relative Income Hypothesis\n**Idea:** Consumption depends on **relative income** (compared to others) and **past peak income.**\n# Two ideas\n- **Demonstration effect:** people imitate consumption of higher income groups.\n- **Ratchet effect:** consumption does not fall easily when income falls.\n[[Ct/Yt = a - b(Yt/Y0)]]\n# Short vs long run\n- Short run: income falls, consumption falls less, APC rises.\n- Long run: income grows steadily, **APC constant**.\n> So Duesenberry explains both budget data and time series, hence **better approach** than Keynes. Criticism: not based on measurable data."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit IV - Money and IS-LM", "q": "What do you mean by IS-LM curve? Discuss how equilibrium is established in IS-LM model.", "y": "2016, 2022, 2023", "a": "# IS-LM Model (Hicks-Hansen)\n**Meaning:** Shows simultaneous equilibrium in goods market (IS) and money market (LM).\n# IS Curve\n- Combination of **interest rate (r) and income (Y)** where **Investment = Saving**.\n- Slopes **downward**: lower r raises investment, raises Y.\n[[I(r) = S(Y)]]\n# LM Curve\n- Combination of r and Y where **money demand = money supply**.\n- Slopes **upward**: higher Y raises transaction demand, r must rise.\n[[M(s) = L(Y, r)]]\n# Equilibrium\n- Where IS and LM intersect, **both markets are in equilibrium**, giving **r* and Y*.**\n- If not at intersection, forces such as excess saving or excess money bring it back.\n# Shifts\n- **Fiscal policy** shifts IS. **Monetary policy** shifts LM.\n- Expansion in G shifts IS right, raising Y and r (crowding out).\n> Conclusion: IS-LM synthesises Keynes and classical views of income and interest."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit V - Growth and Cycles", "q": "Write a note on Hicks' trade cycle theory. Is it an improvement on Samuelson's theory?", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Hicks Trade Cycle Theory (1950)\n**Built on:** multiplier-accelerator interaction, with floor and ceiling.\n# Elements\n- **Autonomous investment** grows at steady rate.\n- **Induced investment** depends on change in output (accelerator).\n- Multiplier raises income, accelerator raises investment.\n# Phases\n- **Upswing:** income rises, accelerator pushes it further.\n- **Ceiling (full employment output):** rise stops, boom ends.\n- **Downswing:** income falls, induced investment falls to zero, **floor** is set by autonomous investment and replacement.\n- Slump ends and recovery begins.\n# Improvement on Samuelson\n- Samuelson gives four types of paths (damped, explosive, steady) but no turning points.\n- Hicks gives **ceiling and floor**, so **fluctuation is bounded and cyclic**.\n> Hence Hicks is considered an improvement. Criticism: ceiling and floor are mechanical."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit I - Trade Theory", "q": "Discuss the doctrine of Reciprocal Demand with the help of offer curves.", "y": "2016, 2022", "a": "# Reciprocal Demand Theory (J.S. Mill)\n**Idea:** Within the limits set by comparative costs, the **terms of trade** are fixed by **the strength of each country's demand for the other's product.**\n# Limits of terms of trade\n- Domestic cost ratios of two countries mark the upper and lower limits.\n# Offer curve (Marshall-Edgeworth)\n- **Offer curve** shows how much of its export a country will give for various quantities of import.\n- Each country has its own offer curve.\n[[Equilibrium = point where the two offer curves intersect]]\n# Working\n- Slope of the line from origin to equilibrium = terms of trade.\n- If a country's demand for the other's good rises, its offer curve shifts and terms of trade become worse for it.\n# Criticism\n- Ignores cost side, two country and two commodity assumption.\n- Does not explain the source of trade.\n> Merit: explains actual terms of trade and gains from trade."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit III - Terms of Trade", "q": "What do you mean by terms of trade? Explain its various kinds and importance.", "y": "2016, 2017, 2022", "a": "# Terms of Trade (TOT)\n**Meaning:** Rate at which a country's exports exchange for imports.\n# Kinds\n- **Net barter TOT:** (Px / Pm) x 100.\n- **Gross barter TOT:** (Qm / Qx) x 100 (Taussig).\n- **Income TOT:** Net barter TOT x export quantity (Dorrance).\n- **Single factoral TOT:** Net barter x productivity index (Viner).\n- **Double factoral TOT:** also includes productivity of foreign factors.\n- **Utility TOT:** (Jacob Viner) gain in real cost and utility.\n[[Net barter TOT = (Export price index / Import price index) x 100]]\n# Importance\n- Shows how gains from trade are divided.\n- Index of trade benefit and welfare.\n- Helps in trade policy and in finding balance of payments position.\n- Prebisch-Singer hypothesis: TOT of developing countries decline over time."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit IV - Exchange Rate", "q": "Critically evaluate the Purchasing Power Parity theory of exchange rate.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Purchasing Power Parity (Gustav Cassel, 1918)\n**Idea:** Exchange rate between two currencies is determined by the **relative purchasing power** of the two currencies.\n# Absolute PPP\n[[R = Pa / Pb]]\n- R = exchange rate, Pa and Pb price levels of country A and B.\n# Relative PPP\n[[R1 = R0 x (Pa1/Pa0) / (Pb1/Pb0)]]\n- Change in exchange rate depends on relative change in price levels.\n# Example\n- If a basket costs Rs 800 in India and $10 in the USA, R = Rs 80 per $.\n# Criticism\n- Ignores **capital flows**, tariffs, transport cost, non-traded goods.\n- Price index differences, taste differences.\n- Reverse causation: exchange rate also affects prices.\n# Merits\n- Simple, useful for long run and for choosing the **new parity after war or devaluation.**"}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit V - BOP and IMF", "q": "What do you mean by Balance of Payments? Discuss the Absorption approach to correct disequilibrium.", "y": "2016, 2018, 2019, 2022", "a": "# Balance of Payments (BOP)\n**Meaning:** Systematic record of all economic transactions between residents of a country and the rest of world in one year.\n- Current account, capital account, official reserve account.\n# Absorption approach (Sidney Alexander, 1952)\n**Idea:** Devaluation improves the trade balance only if **income is more than absorption.**\n[[Y = A + (X - M) ; so B = Y - A]]\n- B = trade balance, Y = income, A = absorption (C + I + G).\n# Working\n- Devaluation raises income through exports, **if there is idle capacity** and income increases more than absorption, B improves.\n- Under full employment, output cannot rise, so **absorption must be cut** by tight fiscal and monetary policy.\n# Direct effects on absorption\n- Cash balance effect, money illusion, redistribution of income, terms of trade effect.\n# Criticism\n- Ignores relative price effect (elasticity), assumes autonomous absorption, difficult to separate income and absorption."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit I - Agriculture", "q": "What do you understand by Agricultural Price Policy? What are its objectives and achievements?", "y": "2018, 2022", "a": "# Agricultural Price Policy (India)\n**Meaning:** Government policy to fix prices of farm products so that farmers get a **remunerative price** and consumers get **reasonable prices**.\n# Institutions\n- **CACP** (Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices) recommends **MSP.**\n- **FCI** buys grain, **PDS** distributes it.\n# Objectives\n- Ensure fair return to farmers, protect from price crash.\n- Encourage adoption of new technology and higher output.\n- Maintain buffer stock, stabilise prices, food security.\n- Balance prices of agriculture and industry (terms of trade).\n# Instruments\n- Minimum Support Price, procurement price, issue price, buffer stock.\n# Achievements\n- Self-sufficiency in wheat and rice, procurement and food security, growth of output.\n# Criticism\n- Focus on wheat and rice, regional imbalance (Punjab, Haryana).\n- Stock accumulation, fiscal burden, neglect of pulses and oilseeds."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit I - Pre-classical", "q": "Critically examine the economic ideas of the Physiocrats.", "y": "2016, 2019", "a": "# Physiocracy (France, 18th century)\n**Meaning:** \"Rule of nature.\" Leader **Francois Quesnay**. Followers: Turgot, Mirabeau.\n# Main ideas\n- **Natural order:** economy governed by natural laws.\n- **Land is the only source of wealth**, agriculture is the only productive activity.\n- **Net product (produit net):** surplus of agriculture over cost.\n- Three classes: **productive** (farmers), **proprietors** (landlords), **sterile** (artisans, merchants).\n- **Tableau Economique (1758):** shows circular flow of income.\n- **Single tax** on net product (land).\n- **Laissez faire, laissez passer.**\n# Criticism\n- Wrongly treats industry and trade as sterile.\n- Too much stress on land. Unrealistic single tax.\n# Contribution\n- First systematic school, idea of circular flow, laissez-faire, influenced Adam Smith."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit I - Classical", "q": "Describe Malthus' ideas on population and over-production (general glut).", "y": "2016, 2019", "a": "# Malthus (1766-1834)\n**Book:** Essay on the Principle of Population (1798).\n# Theory of population\n- **Population grows in geometric ratio** (1, 2, 4, 8) while food grows in **arithmetic ratio** (1, 2, 3, 4).\n- Gap leads to scarcity, so checks are needed.\n- **Preventive checks:** moral restraint, late marriage.\n- **Positive checks:** famine, disease, war.\n# Criticism\n- Technology raised food output, birth rates fell in advanced countries, ignores family planning.\n# Theory of general glut\n- Book: **Principles of Political Economy (1820).**\n- Disagrees with Say's law. **Over-production (glut)** can occur because of **insufficient effective demand.**\n- Solution: unproductive consumers (landlords, state, church) to maintain demand.\n> He anticipated Keynes' idea of effective demand."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit I - Welfare", "q": "Critically analyse the marginalist approach to the theory of the firm.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Marginalist Approach to Theory of the Firm\n**Meaning:** Firm is assumed to be a rational unit that aims at **profit maximisation** by equating **marginal cost and marginal revenue.**\n# Assumptions\n- Single owner-entrepreneur, single product, perfect knowledge, rational behaviour.\n- Short run: fixed plant. Long run: all inputs variable.\n# Equilibrium conditions\n[[First order: MC = MR]]\n[[Second order: slope of MC > slope of MR]]\n- Profit is maximum at output where MC cuts MR from below.\n- Under perfect competition P = MR, so P = MC.\n# Hall and Hitch (1939) criticism\n- Surveyed 38 firms. Found that firms do not know MC and MR, they use **full cost (average cost plus mark-up) pricing.**\n# Other criticisms\n- Ignores other goals: sales maximisation (Baumol), growth (Marris), managerial utility (Williamson), satisficing (Simon).\n- Separation of owner and manager (Berle and Means).\n- Uncertainty, rival reaction and imperfect knowledge.\n# Defence\n- Machlup and Friedman: as-if rational. Model gives correct predictions even if firms do not calculate.\n> Conclusion: Marginalism is a useful base model, but needs to be supplemented by behavioural and managerial theories."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "Explain price leadership and the dominant firm model of price leadership.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Price Leadership in Oligopoly\n**Meaning:** One firm sets the price and other firms follow it. It is a tacit (informal) agreement, so the firms avoid price war.\n# Types\n- **Dominant firm price leadership:** a large firm leads.\n- **Barometric:** a firm that reads market conditions well leads.\n- **Collusive:** leader acts in agreement with others.\n# Dominant Firm Model\n**Assumptions:** One large firm and many small firms. Product is homogeneous. Small firms are price takers.\n# Working\n- Market demand = DD. Small firms supply at the price of the leader (their supply curve = sum of their MCs).\n- **Demand for the leader = market demand minus supply of small firms.**\n- Leader derives its MR from this residual demand curve and produces at **MC = MR.**\n[[Leader output: MC(L) = MR(L)  ; Price = P*]]\n- Small firms take P* and produce where their MC = P*.\n# Result\n- Total supply = leader output + small firms output = market demand at P*.\n# Criticism\n- Leader loses market share in long run, entry of new firms, assumes small firms always follow.\n> Example: steel and oil industries in the past."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit II - Market Structure", "q": "Discuss the nature of oligopoly. Examine Bertrand model of duopoly and say why it is better than Cournot model.", "y": "2020", "a": "# Oligopoly: Nature\n- **Few sellers**, each is big enough to influence price.\n- **Interdependence:** each firm considers rivals' reactions.\n- Products homogeneous or differentiated, barriers to entry, price rigidity, heavy advertisement.\n- **Demand curve is indeterminate** because the reaction of rivals is not known.\n# Bertrand Model (1883)\n**Assumptions:** Two firms, same product and same cost, each assumes the **rival will keep its price constant** (not output), consumers buy from cheaper seller.\n# Working\n- Firm A sets monopoly price. B charges slightly lower and captures the whole market.\n- A then undercuts B. Price cutting continues.\n- It stops where **P = MC** (competitive price).\n[[Bertrand equilibrium: P = MC , profit = 0]]\n# Comparison with Cournot\n- Cournot: output is the decision variable, positive profit. Bertrand: price is the decision variable, zero profit.\n- Bertrand result is the same as perfect competition.\n# Is it better than Cournot?\n- Price is a more realistic decision variable than output, because firms announce prices.\n- But Bertrand ignores capacity limits and product differentiation. Edgeworth showed no stable equilibrium with capacity limits.\n> Conclusion: Bertrand is a useful improvement, but both are partial models."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit V - Growth and Cycles", "q": "Explain Harrod growth model: types of growth rates (warranted, actual, natural).", "y": "2016", "a": "# Harrod Growth Model (1939)\n**Aim:** Find the rate of growth at which economy grows with steady full use of capacity.\n# Assumptions\n- Closed economy, no government, savings proportional to income (s), constant capital-output ratio (C), no depreciation.\n# Three growth rates\n- **Actual rate (G):** s / Ca, where Ca = actual capital-output ratio.\n- **Warranted rate (Gw):** growth rate at which entrepreneurs are satisfied with their investment.\n[[Gw = s / Cr]]\n- **Natural rate (Gn):** maximum rate permitted by population growth and technical progress.\n# Knife-edge problem\n- If **G > Gw**: shortage of capital, investment rises, G moves further above Gw: cumulative boom.\n- If **G < Gw**: excess capacity, investment falls, cumulative depression.\n- Equilibrium is unstable, called the **knife-edge.**\n- For full employment steady growth, **G = Gw = Gn** is required, which is rare.\n# Criticism\n- Fixed capital-output ratio, fixed saving rate, ignores price changes, closed economy.\n> Contribution: dynamic extension of Keynes, base of later growth theories (Solow, Kaldor)."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit V - Growth and Cycles", "q": "Explain Samuelson theory of trade cycle.", "y": "2017", "a": "# Samuelson Multiplier-Accelerator Model (1939)\n**Idea:** Interaction of **multiplier** and **accelerator** creates cycles.\n# Equations\n[[Yt = Ct + It + Gt]]\n[[Ct = c . Y(t-1)  ;  It = v (Ct - C(t-1))]]\n- c = marginal propensity to consume, v = accelerator (capital-output ratio).\n# Four types of paths (depends on c and v)\n- **Damped non-cyclical:** income moves smoothly to new equilibrium.\n- **Damped cycles:** fluctuations die out.\n- **Explosive cycles:** fluctuations grow bigger.\n- **Explosive non-cyclical:** steady rise or fall.\n# Key point\n- If c is small and v is small: damped path. If v is large: explosive cycles.\n# Criticism\n- Fixed c and v, no turning points explained, no ceiling or floor (Hicks later added).\n> Samuelson shows cycles can come from internal forces, but does not explain real turning points."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Unit IV - Money and IS-LM", "q": "What is general equilibrium? Describe any one model of general equilibrium (Walras).", "y": "2019, 2022", "a": "# General Equilibrium (Walras)\n**Meaning:** Simultaneous equilibrium in all markets: product markets, factor markets and money market. Partial equilibrium (Marshall) studies one market only.\n# Walrasian Model\n- Economy has households, firms, commodities and factors.\n- Households maximise utility, firms maximise profit.\n- **Demand for any commodity depends on prices of all commodities and factors.**\n# Equations\n- Number of equations = number of unknown prices and quantities, so system is **determinate**.\n- **Walras' Law:** sum of values of excess demands in all markets is zero.\n[[Sum Pi x Ei = 0]]\n- So if n-1 markets are in equilibrium, the nth is also in equilibrium.\n# Tatonnement\n- Auctioneer calls out prices, adjusts them up or down till excess demand is zero.\n# Conditions\n- Existence, uniqueness and stability (Arrow-Debreu).\n# Criticism\n- Perfect competition, perfect information, no money in early version, unrealistic assumptions, no externalities.\n> Importance: basis of welfare economics and modern equilibrium analysis."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit III - Terms of Trade", "q": "What do you mean by gains from trade? Gains from exchange and gains from specialisation.", "y": "2016, 2018, 2022", "a": "# Gains from Trade\n**Meaning:** Benefits a country gets by trading, compared to no trade (autarky).\n# Two sources\n- **Gains from exchange:** consumers can buy goods at lower price (terms of trade better than domestic ratio), so utility rises even if production stays same.\n- **Gains from specialisation:** countries produce the good in which they have comparative advantage, so world output rises.\n# Explanation (example)\n- Domestic price ratio of India: 1 cloth = 1 wheat. World ratio: 1 cloth = 2 wheat.\n- India exports cloth and gets 2 wheat per cloth instead of 1. The extra wheat is the gain.\n[[Gain = Imports obtained - Imports that would be produced at home cost]]\n# Difference\n- Exchange gain: from consumption, production fixed.\n- Specialisation gain: from production, output rises (shift to a point on higher indifference curve).\n# Division of gains\n- Depends on **terms of trade.** The closer TOT is to the other country domestic ratio, the greater is the gain of the first country (Mill, reciprocal demand).\n# Dynamic gains\n- Economies of scale, technology transfer, competition.\n> Conclusion: Trade benefits both countries but not necessarily equally."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit IV - Exchange Rate", "q": "Discuss the merits and demerits of fixed and flexible exchange rates.", "y": "2016, 2022", "a": "# Exchange Rate Systems\n**Fixed:** rate is fixed by government or central bank (gold standard, Bretton Woods).\n**Flexible (floating):** rate is determined by **demand and supply** in the foreign exchange market.\n# Merits of Fixed Rate\n- Stability and certainty, encourages trade and foreign investment.\n- Checks speculation and inflation, discipline on government.\n# Demerits of Fixed Rate\n- Need large reserves, loss of independent monetary policy.\n- Rigid, disequilibrium persists, black market.\n# Merits of Flexible Rate\n- Automatic correction of BOP, no reserve needed.\n- Independent monetary and fiscal policy.\n- Shock absorber, free from external pressure.\n# Demerits of Flexible Rate\n- Uncertainty, hampers trade and capital flow.\n- Speculation, instability, imported inflation.\n- Developing countries face large fluctuation.\n# Middle way\n- **Managed float** or crawling peg. India follows managed float since 1993.\n> Conclusion: Neither is perfect. A managed system is practical."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit IV - Exchange Rate", "q": "Explain devaluation, its objectives and the Marshall-Lerner condition.", "y": "2018, 2020", "a": "# Devaluation\n**Meaning:** Deliberate reduction in the official value of a currency in terms of foreign currency by the government (under fixed exchange rate).\n# Objectives\n- Correct **BOP deficit**: exports cheaper, imports costlier.\n- Increase exports and employment, protect domestic industry.\n- Help in reaching a new equilibrium rate.\n# Working\n- Price of exports in foreign currency falls, so foreign demand rises. Imports cost more, so import demand falls.\n- Result depends on elasticities.\n# Marshall-Lerner Condition\n[[Ex + Em > 1]]\n- Devaluation improves trade balance only if the **sum of elasticity of demand for exports and imports is greater than one.**\n# J-curve effect\n- In the short run, trade deficit may worsen first (contracts fixed), then improve.\n# Limitations\n- Inflation, retaliation (competitive devaluation), low elasticities, supply constraints.\n# Example\n- India devalued the rupee in 1966 and in 1991.\n> Conclusion: Devaluation works only with elastic demand and spare capacity."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit V - BOP and IMF", "q": "Discuss functions, objectives and achievements of IMF. How has India benefitted?", "y": "2016", "a": "# International Monetary Fund (IMF)\n**Established:** 1944 Bretton Woods, started 1947. Headquarters Washington DC.\n# Objectives\n- Promote international monetary cooperation.\n- Stable exchange rates, orderly adjustments.\n- Balanced growth of trade.\n- Multilateral payment system, remove exchange restrictions.\n- Provide short-term help to member countries in BOP trouble.\n# Functions\n- **Financial:** lending through quotas, Stand-by Arrangements, Extended Fund Facility, SDR allocation.\n- **Regulatory:** exchange rate code of conduct.\n- **Consultative:** Article IV surveillance, technical assistance.\n# Achievements\n- Helped many countries in BOP crises, created **SDR (1969)**.\n- Debt crisis management, standard of economic data.\n# Criticism\n- Conditionality, dominated by rich countries (voting by quota), harsh structural adjustment.\n# India and IMF\n- Founder member, got loans in 1981 (EFF) and 1991 (crisis, gold pledge).\n- Rupee convertibility and reforms were supported.\n> Conclusion: IMF is important for stability, but needs reform in governance."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit V - BOP and IMF", "q": "Distinguish between Foreign Direct Investment and Portfolio Investment.", "y": "2018, 2022", "a": "# FDI and FPI\n**FDI:** Investment by a foreign firm in physical assets with **lasting interest and control** in an enterprise (at least 10 percent equity).\n**FPI:** Investment in shares, bonds, mutual funds for **returns only**, no control (below 10 percent).\n# Differences\n- **Control:** FDI gives management control. FPI does not.\n- **Time:** FDI is long-term. FPI is short-term and volatile (hot money).\n- **Nature:** FDI brings capital, technology, management. FPI only finance.\n- **Impact:** FDI creates jobs and output. FPI affects stock market and exchange rate.\n- **Risk:** FDI is stable. FPI can reverse quickly, causing a crisis (1997 Asian crisis).\n- **Entry:** FDI route in India via automatic or approval route. FPI via registered FPIs.\n# Merits and demerits\n- FDI: technology transfer, exports, but profit repatriation, dominance of MNCs.\n- FPI: liquidity, cost of capital, but volatility.\n> Conclusion: FDI is preferred for development, FPI needs prudent control."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit I - Trade Theory", "q": "Discuss opportunity cost theory of Haberler.", "y": "2020", "a": "# Haberler's Opportunity Cost Theory (1933)\n**Background:** Ricardo used labour theory of value, which has defects. Haberler restated comparative advantage in terms of **opportunity cost.**\n# Concept\n- Opportunity cost of a good = **amount of another good that has to be given up** to produce one more unit.\n# Production Possibility Curve (PPC)\n- Slope of PPC shows opportunity cost (MRT).\n- Under constant cost: PPC is a straight line. Under increasing cost: concave.\n# Comparative advantage\n- A country has an advantage in a good if its opportunity cost for that good is lower.\n- Country exports the good of lower opportunity cost.\n[[Trade is mutually beneficial when opportunity costs differ]]\n# Merits\n- Includes many factors, not only labour. Explains gains with PPC and indifference curves.\n- Realistic, increasing costs.\n# Criticism\n- Does not explain why costs differ (Heckscher-Ohlin does).\n- Static, ignores transport cost and tariffs.\n> Conclusion: It is a generalisation of Ricardian theory."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit V - BOP and IMF", "q": "What are Regional Trade Agreements? Discuss their effects.", "y": "2018", "a": "# Regional Trade Agreements (RTAs)\n**Meaning:** Agreements among a group of countries to reduce or remove trade barriers within the group.\n# Forms (stages)\n- **Preferential Trade Area**\n- **Free Trade Area** (NAFTA, SAFTA)\n- **Customs Union** (common external tariff)\n- **Common Market** (free movement of factors)\n- **Economic Union** (EU)\n# Effects (Viner)\n- **Trade creation:** shift from high-cost home producer to low-cost partner. Raises welfare.\n- **Trade diversion:** shift from low-cost outsider to high-cost partner. Lowers welfare.\n# Merits\n- Larger market, economies of scale, investment, cooperation.\n# Demerits\n- Discrimination against outsiders, may weaken WTO multilateralism.\n# India\n- SAFTA, ASEAN-India FTA, CEPA with South Korea and Japan.\n> Conclusion: RTAs are a second-best choice, net effect depends on creation versus diversion."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit I - Agriculture", "q": "Discuss the National Agriculture Policy and causes of low agricultural growth.", "y": "2016, 2019", "a": "# National Agriculture Policy, 2000\n**Aim:** Sustainable, resource-efficient, demand-driven agriculture, with growth rate of **more than 4 percent per year.**\n# Main features\n- Better use of land, water and bio-resources.\n- Value addition, agro-processing and rural industries.\n- Credit, insurance and marketing support to farmers.\n- Price protection (MSP), reduce import risk, promote exports.\n- Research and extension, strengthening of cooperatives.\n# Causes of low agricultural growth\n- **Dependence on monsoon,** only about half of area is irrigated.\n- **Small and fragmented holdings,** low scale economies.\n- Low productivity, poor use of technology, inadequate seeds and fertiliser.\n- **Low public investment** in irrigation, roads, storage.\n- Weak credit and marketing, role of middlemen, price fluctuation.\n- Land reforms incomplete, tenancy problems.\n# Measures\n- More irrigation, crop insurance (PMFBY), e-NAM, soil health card, MSP reforms, diversification.\n> Conclusion: Growth needs investment, technology and market reforms together."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit II - Industry", "q": "Discuss the major components of Industrial Policy development (1956, 1991).", "y": "2016, 2020", "a": "# Industrial Policy in India\n**Meaning:** Government rules and strategy for industrial development.\n# Industrial Policy Resolution 1948\n- Mixed economy. Industries in 4 groups: state monopoly (arms, atomic energy, railways), state and private, regulated private, private.\n# Industrial Policy Resolution 1956 (Economic Constitution of India)\n- Socialist pattern of society.\n- **Schedule A:** 17 industries exclusively for the state.\n- **Schedule B:** 12 industries, mixed.\n- **Schedule C:** rest, left to private sector.\n- Promotion of small scale and cottage industries, balanced regional growth.\n# 1977 and 1980\n- Stress on small scale sector, District Industries Centres, partial liberalisation.\n# New Industrial Policy 1991\n- **Abolished industrial licensing,** except for 18 industries.\n- Public sector reserved list reduced to 8 industries (now very few).\n- **MRTP limits removed.**\n- Automatic permission for foreign investment (up to 51 percent), foreign technology.\n- Disinvestment of PSUs.\n# Evaluation\n- Greater competition, growth of service and industry, but jobless growth, weak MSMEs, inequalities.\n> Conclusion: Policy shifted from state control to market orientation."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit III - Poverty and Population", "q": "Define poverty. Explain the strategy adopted by India to remove poverty.", "y": "2020, 2022", "a": "# Poverty\n**Meaning:** Condition where a person cannot meet basic needs of food, clothing, shelter, health and education.\n- **Absolute poverty:** below a poverty line, measured by minimum calorie or expenditure.\n- **Relative poverty:** compared with others in society.\n- In India, measured as **head count ratio** (Tendulkar method gave about 21.9 percent in 2011-12).\n# Strategy to remove poverty\n**1. Growth-oriented**\n- Higher GDP growth, agriculture and industry growth.\n**2. Direct anti-poverty programmes**\n- Self-employment: IRDP (earlier), SGSY, **NRLM**, PM Mudra.\n- Wage employment: **MGNREGA (2005):** 100 days of work.\n- Food and nutrition: **PDS, National Food Security Act 2013,** mid-day meal.\n- Housing and basic needs: PM Awas, Jal Jeevan.\n**3. Social security**\n- Pension, insurance, direct benefit transfer, **Jan Dhan Yojana.**\n**4. Structural measures**\n- Land reforms, education and health, decentralisation.\n# Evaluation\n- Leakages, targeting errors, weak implementation, regional gap.\n> Conclusion: Growth combined with employment, human development and good delivery is needed."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit III - Poverty and Population", "q": "Write a note on development of population policy in India.", "y": "2016, 2017", "a": "# Population Policy of India\n**Background:** Population is more than 140 crore. India was the first country to announce a family planning programme (1952).\n# Stages\n- **1952:** Family Planning Programme started.\n- **1976:** First National Population Policy: raised minimum age of marriage (18 for girls, 21 for boys), incentives, sterilisation drive. Compulsion in Emergency created a backlash.\n- **1977:** Name changed to **Family Welfare Programme** (voluntary).\n- **1983:** National Health Policy, goal of replacement fertility by 2000.\n- **2000: National Population Policy** (current).\n# National Population Policy 2000\n- **Immediate:** meet unmet needs for contraception, health infrastructure.\n- **Medium (2010):** bring TFR to replacement level (2.1).\n- **Long-term (2045):** stabilise population.\n- Targets: IMR below 30, MMR below 100, universal immunisation, 100 percent registration of births and marriages, delay marriage.\n# Evaluation\n- TFR has fallen to about 2.0 (NFHS-5). Still high momentum, regional gaps (UP, Bihar).\n> Conclusion: Voluntary approach with education, health and women empowerment is effective."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit IV - Environment and Energy", "q": "Outline National Environment Policy of India.", "y": "2020, 2022", "a": "# National Environment Policy, 2006\n**Aim:** Protect environment and also meet development needs: **sustainable development.**\n# Objectives\n- Conservation of critical resources.\n- Intra- and inter-generational equity.\n- Integration of environment in development.\n- Efficient use of resources, good environmental governance.\n# Principles\n- **Polluter pays,** precautionary principle, public trust, economic efficiency.\n# Legal framework\n- Wildlife Protection Act 1972, Water Act 1974, Forest Conservation Act 1980, **Air Act 1981, Environment Protection Act 1986,** Biodiversity Act 2002, **National Green Tribunal 2010.**\n# Climate action\n- **NAPCC 2008** with 8 missions (solar, energy efficiency, green India etc.).\n- India NDCs, target of net zero by 2070, 500 GW non-fossil capacity by 2030.\n# Challenges\n- Pollution, deforestation, weak enforcement, urban waste.\n> Conclusion: Need to balance growth with ecological sustainability."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit IV - Environment and Energy", "q": "Why is energy policy essential? Outline India energy policy.", "y": "2019", "a": "# Energy Policy: Need\n- Energy is a basic input for growth, industry, agriculture, transport.\n- India imports a large share of oil, so **energy security** is a concern.\n- Need for universal access, clean energy and climate commitment.\n# Integrated Energy Policy 2006\n- Meet energy demand of all sectors at lowest cost, with security and sustainability.\n- Efficient markets, reduce subsidy, competition, energy efficiency.\n# Main elements\n- **Coal:** largest source, push for clean coal.\n- **Oil and gas:** exploration (NELP, HELP), strategic reserves.\n- **Electricity:** Electricity Act 2003, rural electrification (Saubhagya), UDAY.\n- **Renewables:** National Solar Mission (2010), target **500 GW non-fossil by 2030,** wind, hydro, green hydrogen.\n- **Nuclear:** Indo-US civil nuclear deal.\n- **Efficiency:** BEE star rating, LED (UJALA).\n# Problems\n- Coal dependence, losses of discoms, import bills, land and finance for renewables.\n> Conclusion: Move towards a balanced mix, with more renewable energy."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit V - External Sector", "q": "Discuss main features of India foreign trade policy.", "y": "2018, 2019", "a": "# Foreign Trade Policy (FTP)\n**Meaning:** Government policy on exports and imports, issued by **DGFT (Ministry of Commerce)** under the Foreign Trade Act 1992.\n# Main features\n- **Export promotion:** incentives, simplified procedures, market access.\n- **Schemes:** EPCG (zero duty import of capital goods), Advance Authorisation, **RoDTEP** (rebate of taxes), SEZ, EOU, Duty Drawback.\n- **Liberalised imports:** most items freely importable, few restricted.\n- **Trade facilitation:** online licensing, single window, e-BRC.\n- **Services export:** support for IT and other services.\n- **FTP 2023:** target of **2 trillion dollars exports by 2030,** Districts as Export Hubs, Towns of Export Excellence, merchant exporters, dispute settlement, e-commerce hubs.\n# Evaluation\n- Exports and services grew, but trade deficit remains because of oil imports, concentration of products and markets.\n> Conclusion: Competitive quality and diversification are essential for export success."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit V - External Sector", "q": "Capital account convertibility: arguments for and against.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Capital Account Convertibility (CAC)\n**Meaning:** Freedom to convert local currency into foreign currency and vice versa for **capital transactions** (investment, loans), without restrictions.\n- India has current account convertibility (since 1994) and **partial** capital account convertibility.\n# Tarapore Committee (1997, 2006)\n- Suggested pre-conditions: fiscal deficit 3.5 percent of GDP, inflation 3-5 percent, NPAs 5 percent, strong banks, adequate reserves.\n# Arguments in favour\n- Free flow of capital, more investment, lower cost of capital.\n- Portfolio diversification, integration with world, discipline on policy.\n# Arguments against\n- **Volatility and hot money:** sudden outflow causes crisis (East Asia 1997).\n- Exchange rate instability, loss of monetary independence.\n- Weak financial system, risk to small economies.\n# India stand\n- Gradual approach, limits on FPI and ECB.\n> Conclusion: Move step by step after financial and fiscal strength."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit I - Agriculture", "q": "What is food security? Outline India food security policy.", "y": "2018", "a": "# Food Security\n**Meaning (FAO):** All people at all times have physical and economic access to sufficient, safe and nutritious food. Dimensions: **availability, access, utilisation, stability.**\n# India Food Security Policy\n- **Buffer stock** by FCI, **MSP** and procurement.\n- **PDS (Public Distribution System)** through fair price shops.\n- **National Food Security Act, 2013:** covers up to 75 percent rural and 50 percent urban population, **5 kg per person per month** of subsidised grain (Rs 3, 2, 1 per kg), Antyodaya households 35 kg, maternity benefit, meal for children.\n- **PM Garib Kalyan Anna Yojana:** free grain.\n- Mid-day meal, ICDS, TPDS.\n# Problems\n- Leakages and diversion, storage losses, regional imbalance, focus on rice and wheat, nutrition (not only calories).\n# Reforms\n- Digitised ration cards, Aadhaar linking, One Nation One Ration Card, DBT.\n> Conclusion: Food security needs production, storage and efficient delivery."}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit II - Classical and Socialist", "q": "Describe in brief the economic ideas of Karl Marx.", "y": "2016, 2019", "a": ""}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit I - Ancient and Indian", "q": "Describe the economic ideas of Kautilya.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": ""}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit V - Indian Thought", "q": "Explain the economic ideas of M. K. Gandhi.", "y": "2019, 2022", "a": ""}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit III - Neo-classical", "q": "What is Neo-classical economics? Explain its contributions.", "y": "2017, 2019, 2022", "a": ""}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit III - Neo-classical", "q": "Discuss the contribution of Marshall to economic thought.", "y": "2017, 2020", "a": ""}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit IV - Modern", "q": "How is Keynesian theory of employment different from the classical theory?", "y": "2020, 2022", "a": ""}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit IV - Modern", "q": "Evaluate contributions of Wicksell and Pigou.", "y": "2018", "a": ""}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit V - Indian Thought", "q": "Critically estimate J. K. Mehta theory of wantlessness.", "y": "2018, 2020, 2022", "a": ""}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit II - Classical and Socialist", "q": "Critically examine economic ideas of Saint-Simon. Assess the contribution of Proudhon.", "y": "2020, 2022", "a": ""}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit V - Indian Thought", "q": "Discuss main economic ideas of Nehru and compare Nehruism with Gandhism.", "y": "2020", "a": ""}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit III - Neo-classical", "q": "Explain economic ideas of Walras (general equilibrium).", "y": "2017", "a": ""}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit IV - Modern", "q": "What is the Cobweb Model? Explain its types.", "y": "2020", "a": ""}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit III - Neo-classical", "q": "Explain Cobb-Douglas production function.", "y": "2022", "a": ""}, {"p": "P4 History of Economic Thought", "u": "Unit V - Indian Thought", "q": "Write the outline of economic ideas of R. C. Dutt.", "y": "2017", "a": ""}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Short Notes (Q1-B)", "q": "Write a short note on Pigou Effect.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Pigou Effect\n**Meaning:** Fall in price level raises the **real value of money balances**, which raises wealth and consumption.\n- Given by A. C. Pigou against Keynes' under-employment equilibrium.\n- Prices fall in depression, so real balances rise, people spend more, aggregate demand rises, full employment returns.\n- Also called **real balance effect** (Patinkin).\n# Criticism\n- Fall in prices hurts debtors, expectations of further fall delay spending, rigid wages.\n> Importance: shows that classical self-correction is possible, in theory."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Short Notes (Q1-B)", "q": "Write a short note on Demonstration Effect.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Demonstration Effect\n**Meaning:** People imitate the consumption pattern of higher income groups or of richer countries.\n- Given by **Duesenberry.**\n- Individual consumption depends on the **consumption of neighbours**, not only on own income.\n- Raises propensity to consume, lowers savings and capital formation.\n- Important in developing countries: imitation of western standards.\n# Criticism\n- Hard to measure.\n> It explains why APC does not fall in long run."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Short Notes (Q1-B)", "q": "Write a short note on Selling Costs.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Selling Costs\n**Meaning:** Expenses incurred to **create or increase demand** for a product, e.g. advertisement, salesmen, samples.\n- Concept of **Chamberlin**, in monopolistic competition.\n- Selling costs shift the demand curve to the right and make it less elastic.\n- Production costs: meet existing demand. Selling costs: change demand.\n- Selling cost curve is **U-shaped.**\n[[Total cost = Production cost + Selling cost]]\n> Firm spends till MR from extra sales = marginal selling cost."}, {"p": "P1 Economic Analysis", "u": "Short Notes (Q1-B)", "q": "Write a short note on Reaction Curve.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Reaction Curve\n**Meaning:** Curve showing how a firm changes its output as the **output of rival changes**, to maximise profit.\n- Used in Cournot duopoly and Stackelberg model.\n- Reaction curve of firm A: A best output for each output of B. Slopes **downward.**\n- Reaction curves of A and B intersect at **Cournot equilibrium.**\n[[Cournot equilibrium = intersection of reaction curves]]\n> If B increases output, A decreases output; if B decreases, A increases."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit I - Trade Theory", "q": "Distinguish between internal and international trade. Explain the absolute advantage theory of trade.", "y": "2016", "a": "# Internal and International Trade\n- **Mobility of factors:** high within country, low between countries.\n- **Currency:** one currency inside, many currencies between countries.\n- **Barriers:** tariffs, quotas between countries.\n- **Markets:** homogeneous inside, differences in laws, language, culture outside.\n- **Theory:** classical economists say separate theory is needed for international trade.\n# Absolute Advantage Theory (Adam Smith, 1776)\n**Idea:** Country should produce and export the good it can produce with **less labour cost than other country** (absolute advantage) and import the rest.\n# Example\n- Labour hours per unit: India makes cloth in 10 hours, wheat in 20. UK makes cloth in 20, wheat in 10.\n- India specialises in cloth, UK in wheat, and both gain by exchange.\n# Assumptions\n- Two countries, two goods, labour only factor, no transport cost, free trade.\n# Criticism\n- Cannot explain trade when one country has absolute advantage in all goods. (Ricardo solved this by **comparative advantage.**)\n> Contribution: Basis of free trade and division of labour."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit I - Trade Theory", "q": "Explain the Heckscher-Ohlin theory of international trade.", "y": "2018, 2019", "a": "# Heckscher-Ohlin (Factor Proportions) Theory\n**Given by:** Eli Heckscher (1919), Bertil Ohlin (1933).\n# Main idea\n- Trade arises because of **differences in factor endowments.**\n- A country exports the good that uses its **abundant factor** intensively and imports the good that uses its scarce factor.\n# Assumptions\n- Two countries, two goods, two factors (labour, capital), same technology, constant returns, perfect competition, no transport cost, factor immobile between countries.\n# Explanation\n- Capital abundant country (USA): capital cheap, so exports capital-intensive goods.\n- Labour abundant country (India): labour cheap, exports labour-intensive goods.\n[[Factor price difference -> Commodity price difference -> Trade]]\n# Factor Price Equalisation (Samuelson)\n- Trade tends to equalise relative factor prices in the two countries.\n# Leontief Paradox (1953)\n- US exports were less capital-intensive than imports, against the theory.\n# Criticism\n- Same technology unrealistic, demand differences, ignores economies of scale.\n> Conclusion: Modern theory but empirical support is mixed."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit V - BOP and IMF", "q": "What are the causes of disequilibrium in Balance of Payments? Explain measures to correct it.", "y": "2016, 2019, 2022", "a": "# BOP Disequilibrium\n**Meaning:** When autonomous receipts and payments are not equal, i.e. deficit or surplus in BOP.\n# Causes\n- **Cyclical:** boom or recession.\n- **Structural:** change in demand or supply pattern, supply bottlenecks.\n- **Secular:** long-term, as in developing countries (rising import needs).\n- **Others:** inflation, high imports of capital goods, population pressure, political instability, capital flight.\n# Measures to correct\n**Monetary**\n- Credit squeeze, higher bank rate, to reduce inflation and imports.\n**Fiscal**\n- Cut government spending, raise taxes.\n**Exchange rate**\n- Devaluation or depreciation to raise exports.\n**Trade policy**\n- Export promotion: subsidies, EPZ, SEZ.\n- Import control: tariffs, quotas, import substitution.\n**Others**\n- Foreign loans and aid, attract FDI, exchange control, IMF help.\n# Evaluation\n- Deflation hurts growth, devaluation needs elasticity, import control invites retaliation.\n> Conclusion: A mix of expenditure-reducing and expenditure-switching policies is best."}, {"p": "P2 International Economics", "u": "Unit IV - Exchange Rate", "q": "Discuss the merits and demerits of floating exchange rate system.", "y": "2022", "a": "# Floating Exchange Rate\n**Meaning:** Exchange rate is set by market demand and supply, with no or little government intervention. **Clean float:** no intervention. **Dirty (managed) float:** central bank sometimes intervenes.\n# Determination\n[[Equilibrium rate: Demand for forex = Supply of forex]]\n- Rise in demand for foreign currency depreciates the home currency.\n# Merits\n- BOP adjusts automatically.\n- No need of large reserves.\n- Freedom in domestic policy (monetary and fiscal).\n- Prevents foreign shocks, no black market.\n# Demerits\n- Exchange rate uncertainty affects trade and investment.\n- Speculative activity causes sharp fluctuation.\n- May cause inflation (depreciation raises import prices).\n- Harms small and developing countries with weak currency.\n# India\n- Managed floating system since 1993 (market-determined rate with RBI intervention).\n> Conclusion: Practical for open economies with strong financial markets."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit V - External Sector", "q": "What were the main reforms of the New Economic Policy 1991? Evaluate them.", "y": "2016, 2017, 2020", "a": "# Background of 1991 Reforms\n- Balance of payments crisis, foreign reserves enough for 2 weeks of imports, high inflation, fiscal deficit. India pledged gold and took IMF help.\n# Components (LPG)\n**Liberalisation**\n- Industrial licensing abolished, MRTP limits removed, rupee devalued (July 1991), import tariff cut.\n**Privatisation**\n- Disinvestment of PSUs, reserved list cut.\n**Globalisation**\n- FDI up to 51 percent automatic, trade liberalisation, rupee made convertible on current account (1994).\n# Sectoral reforms\n- **Financial:** Narasimham Committee, SLR and CRR cut, private banks.\n- **Tax:** Chelliah Committee, lower rates, VAT, later GST.\n- **External:** FEMA replaced FERA.\n# Evaluation\n- Growth, forex reserves, IT and service boom, poverty fell.\n- But rising inequality, jobless growth, agriculture neglect.\n> Conclusion: Reforms transformed the economy but inclusion remains a challenge."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit I - Agriculture", "q": "What is land reform? Discuss the measures and their evaluation.", "y": "2016, 2018", "a": "# Land Reforms\n**Meaning:** Changes in ownership and operation of land to remove inequality and raise productivity.\n# Measures\n- **Abolition of intermediaries** (zamindari, jagirdari). About 2 crore tenants became owners.\n- **Tenancy reforms:** fair rent, security of tenure, ownership to tenants.\n- **Ceiling on holdings:** surplus land distributed to landless.\n- **Consolidation of holdings.**\n- **Bhoodan and Gramdan** (Vinoba Bhave).\n- Land records digitisation.\n# Evaluation\n- **Success:** zamindari abolished, West Bengal and Kerala achieved tenancy gains.\n- **Failures:** loopholes in ceiling laws (benami transfers), poor records, weak political will, tenants evicted.\n# Impact\n- Equity, productivity, better credit. But tenant insecurity remains.\n> Conclusion: Better records, tenancy legalisation and cooperative farming are needed."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit I - Agriculture", "q": "Explain the Green Revolution. What are its achievements and limitations?", "y": "2016, 2019", "a": "# Green Revolution\n**Meaning:** Rapid rise in food-grain output due to **high yielding varieties (HYV), fertiliser, irrigation and technology.** Started 1966-67. Led by **Norman Borlaug** abroad and **M. S. Swaminathan** in India.\n# Components\n- HYV seeds (wheat, rice), chemical fertilisers, irrigation, pesticides, credit, MSP, research.\n# Achievements\n- Food grain production rose from about 50 million tonnes (1950-51) to over 300 million tonnes.\n- Self-sufficiency, end of PL-480 imports, buffer stock.\n- Rural employment, agro-industry growth.\n# Limitations\n- **Regional inequality** (Punjab, Haryana, Western UP).\n- **Crop inequality** (mainly wheat and rice, pulses neglected).\n- Farm inequality: large farmers gained more.\n- Environmental harm: groundwater fall, soil degradation, chemicals.\n- Dependence on inputs and subsidy.\n# Way forward\n- Evergreen revolution, sustainable farming, second green revolution in eastern India.\n> Conclusion: A great success in output but needs sustainability and inclusion."}, {"p": "P3 India Economic Policy", "u": "Unit III - Poverty and Population", "q": "Discuss the problem of unemployment in India. What measures have been taken?", "y": "2017, 2020", "a": "# Unemployment in India\n**Types:** Open, **disguised** (in agriculture), seasonal, **structural**, cyclical, educated unemployment, underemployment.\n# Causes\n- Rapid population growth, slow industrial growth, **jobless growth,** dependence on agriculture.\n- Weak skills, mismatch of education and jobs.\n- Inadequate capital, small scale of services.\n# Effects\n- Poverty, social unrest, wastage of human resources.\n# Measures\n- **MGNREGA 2005:** 100 days guaranteed wage employment.\n- **Skill India,** PMKVY, Start-up India, Stand-up India, **Mudra loans.**\n- NRLM, PM Employment Generation Programme.\n- Make in India, MSME support, infrastructure projects, labour code reforms.\n# Evaluation\n- Programmes help, but formal sector job creation is low.\n> Conclusion: Labour-intensive growth with skill development is the lasting solution."}];
